{"kind":"report","text":"Disposition of the checker's request 66856a8b: confirmed, one combined row. Under the adopted contract revision 23 a mark that is not a binding selector may not carry index or key, so the destination's withdraw is delivered as one row or not at all, in the authority note and in the source alike. That row holds together: the item type publication declares its index on the operation slot; the selector publication-of states that index and the message's operation field as its key, the field's type equal to the slot's; the handler withdraw states bound, with of naming what the selector selects and with its where conditions; a publication's holds states decisions with withdraw at 1; in a bound delivery the rule open-withdrawn opens nothing; and where no publication exists for the operation, the request is new work and its rule opens the publication in its initial state with the required fixed slots and then sets the state not-reserved, with the rule's most restated to those effects, as my disposition 9d2d137f records. Authority revision 28, now in review, states the index and key rows as taking effect only with a contract revision that carries them and still carries the bound, decisions and no-open rows in its carried table; it does not claim that an index-only version validates, and the note's next revision (29) will state the whole row at once now that revision 23 is adopted. For the source: the fourth milestone builds the generic index, selector, bound and decisions forms on made-up labelled data only; the real publication type gains none of them until the one combined row is adopted, and then all of it lands in one commit with its witness. Kept owned and not decided here: the disposition of withdrawDecided, and the publication's reservation counts with the marker duty for the compromised notice (the sum of 248 further entries recorded in revision 27). No source or execution claim is made."}